@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+Conditional yes to ONE frozen validation; no parameter changes. The close-entry audit did not break the result. However, the declared family maximum-PF bootstrap remains outstanding: neither single-version null completes that guard. Finish it on dev before giving a protocol-compliant validation go. Validation was not accessed.
+
+All performance numbers below are **dev, in-sample, selected from 13,824 versions**.
+
+1. **Execution and timing.** Independent replay reproduced 1,281 trades, $61,525 net, PF 1.309.
+
+   - 1,206 trades filled on the next minute bar. Of these, 335 opened strictly through the limit: PF 1.228. Across all fill bars, 346 received price improvement; removing those trades leaves PF 1.352. Improvement totals 497 ticks/$4,970; subtracting it from P&L, without changing exits, leaves PF 1.280. No fill opened beyond its stop. A full engine rerun delaying order activation one additional minute gives 1,241 trades, PF 1.322. This does not model queue priority or subminute latency.
+   - Of 91 entries at/after 14:00 CT, 90 flatten and one stops; net $2,152. Excluding late signals and rerunning gives 1,190 trades, PF 1.303. They are mostly forced-exit bets, but do not explain the edge.
+   - Across 1,393 orders: zero signals before hourly completion, zero hourly-close mismatches, zero cross-session or cross-contract signal candles. There are 31 delayed completions because the final minute is missing; their 28 filled trades lose $893, PF 0.800. Candles with fewer than 60 observed minute bars produce 289 trades, PF 1.128; complete candles produce 992, PF 1.362.
+   - Early-ending observed sessions produce 40 trades, PF 1.924; excluding them leaves PF 1.292. This is a holiday/data-gap proxy, not a verified holiday calendar. Within ±3 calendar days of contract switches: 177 trades, PF 1.611; outside: 1,104, PF 1.268. Five trades exit explicitly at roll. Removing all flagged trades together—improved fills, late entries, early-ending sessions, roll windows, incomplete candles—leaves 599 trades, PF 1.328. These exclusions are diagnostics, not proposed filters.
+
+2. **Stronger null.** The random-date null preserves clock minute but breaks daily volatility, directional conditions and bracket-risk compatibility. I instead relocate each order to another completed hour on the same trading day, same side, same side-adjusted CLV ≥0.6, and same broad time bucket: overnight, 08:30–13:59, or 14:00–15:09 CT. Keep original risk ticks, 1R target, expiry, fees and engine rules; remove the level condition. Deduplicate colliding signals and enforce one position as usual.
+
+   1,279/1,393 orders have another eligible hour; the matched real subset gives 1,187 trades, PF 1.357. Across 300 null runs: median 1,176 trades; PF median **0.845**, central 95% **0.774–0.919**, maximum **0.948**; **0/300** reach either 1.309 or 1.357. Conditioning more closely makes this control more relevant, not necessarily more profitable. It still does not preserve exact local ATR or band-derived geometry. Zero exceedances gives Monte Carlo p=1/301≈0.0033, not a family-adjusted significance result. Do not interpret z=5.2 as selection-adjusted evidence.
+
+3. **Freeze and sizing.** Keep the exact version and existing validation gates (PF≥1.10, pass≥40%); add no time, touch, holiday or roll filters. Flat versus TF weights changes effective selectivity, so the weaker sibling does not cleanly isolate weighting's value.
+
+   Use **one CL at most**, never automatically five. Planned one-CL stop loss including fees: median **$374**, 95th percentile **$924**, maximum **$2,354**. At five CL, **581/1,281** planned losses exceed the starting $2,000 cushion. Even one CL is unsuitable for some signals. For eventual execution, size against remaining MLL cushion; my conservative cap would be 10% including fees/slippage, with zero contracts meaning skip. Only 89 historical trades meet $200 planned risk before slippage. That cap creates a separate variant and cannot inherit the reported 52% pass rate. Topstep confirms the $2,000 MLL trails EOD balance but is enforced intraday, including open P&L: https://help.topstep.com/en/articles/8284204-what-is-the-maximum-loss-limit
+
+Artifacts: round3_audit.py, round3_summary.json, round3_trade_audit.csv, round3_orders.csv, round3_same_day_null.csv, round3_followup.py/json, logs and source hashes, all in this directory. Python processes ran sequentially under ulimit -v 3000000. The initial memory-limited attempt stopped with MemoryError; the successful audit uses equivalent process-local timestamp indexing to avoid object expansion and drops unused columns. No production sources were edited; no swing-stack snapshots were stored.
